**Interpretation.** In the problem, $\mathbf{CRing}$ denotes the category of commutative rings *not necessarily possessing a unit* (i.e. commutative rngs), and $\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}$ is the (non-full) subcategory of *unital* commutative rings: objects are rngs $R$ equipped with a unit element $1_R\in R$, equivalently equipped with a **unit map**
$$\eta_R:\mathbb Z\longrightarrow R,\qquad n\longmapsto n\cdot 1_R,$$
and morphisms $f:R\to S$ are rng homomorphisms *preserving the unit*, i.e. $f\circ\eta_R=\eta_S$. The equivalence $\mathbf{Aff}\simeq \mathbf{CRing}^{\mathrm{op}}$ is the contravariant spectrum functor
$$A\longmapsto \operatorname{Spec} A,\qquad (A\xrightarrow{\varphi}B)\longmapsto (\operatorname{Spec}B\xrightarrow{{}^{a}\varphi}\operatorname{Spec} A).$$

---

**Claim.** Under $\mathbf{Aff}\simeq\mathbf{CRing}^{\mathrm{op}}$, the subcategory $\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}^{\mathrm{op}}$ corresponds to the **slice category $\mathbf{Aff}_{/\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z}$ of affine schemes over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$** (affine schemes equipped with a structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$, and morphisms compatible with it). Equivalently, it is the ordinary category of affine schemes.

---

**Proof.**

*Objects.* An object of $\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}$ is a rng $R$ together with a unit map $\eta_R:\mathbb Z\to R$ (a morphism in $\mathbf{CRing}$, since $\mathbb Z$ is itself a unital rng). Applying the equivalence, the pair $(R,\eta_R)$ is sent to the affine scheme $\operatorname{Spec}R$ together with the scheme morphism
$${}^{a}\eta_R:\operatorname{Spec}R\longrightarrow \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z.$$
Conversely, an affine scheme $\operatorname{Spec}R$ (for a rng $R$) equipped with a morphism $p:\operatorname{Spec}R\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$ arises from a rng homomorphism $\mathbb Z\to R$, i.e. from a unit map, hence from a unital ring structure on $R$. Thus the objects of $\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}^{\mathrm{op}}$ are exactly the affine schemes over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$.

*Morphisms.* A morphism $f:R\to S$ in $\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}$ is a rng homomorphism satisfying $f\circ\eta_R=\eta_S$. Under the equivalence it becomes a scheme morphism ${}^{a}f:\operatorname{Spec}S\to\operatorname{Spec}R$, and the unit-preservation condition translates, by contravariance, into the commutativity
$$\operatorname{Spec}S\xrightarrow{{}^{a}f}\operatorname{Spec}R\xrightarrow{{}^{a}\eta_R}\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z \;=\; \operatorname{Spec}S\xrightarrow{{}^{a}\eta_S}\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z,$$
i.e. ${}^{a}f$ is a morphism **over** $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$. Conversely, any scheme morphism $\operatorname{Spec}S\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$ comes from a rng map $R\to S$ preserving the unit maps, hence from a morphism of $\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}$.

*Conclusion.* The equivalence $\mathbf{Aff}\simeq\mathbf{CRing}^{\mathrm{op}}$ restricts to an equivalence
$$\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}^{\mathrm{op}}\;\simeq\;\mathbf{Aff}_{/\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z}.$$
Geometrically, "having a unit" is precisely the data of a structure morphism to the final affine scheme $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$: the unit $1_R\in R$ is the image of $1\in\mathbb Z$ under the unit map, and on spectra this is the canonical morphism to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$. Hence the subcategory of affine schemes corresponding to $\mathbf{CRing}_{\star}^{\mathrm{op}}$ is **the category of affine schemes over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$** — the ordinary category of affine schemes, each equipped with its canonical structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$. $\blacksquare$

